.TH BRK 2 "2026-10-18" "Substrate" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
brk, sbrk \- change the end of the data segment
.SH LIBRARY
Substrate libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <unistd.h>

int brk(void *addr);
void *sbrk(intptr_t increment);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I program break
is the end of the process's heap: the first address after its data
segment.
It starts at the end of the program's loaded image.
.P
.BR brk ()
moves the break to
.IR addr .
Growing the heap maps new zero-filled pages; shrinking it gives the pages
above the new break back to the system.
.P
.BR sbrk ()
moves the break by
.I increment
bytes and returns the old break, so the new memory begins at the value
returned.
.BR sbrk (0)
returns the current break without changing it.
.P
Both are built on the
.B SYS_BRK
system call, which takes the requested break (0 to query) and returns the
break in effect afterwards.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR brk ()
returns 0 on success.
On error it returns \-1 and sets
.IR errno .
.P
.BR sbrk ()
returns the previous break on success.
On error it returns
.B (void *) \-1
and sets
.IR errno .
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The break could not be moved: the requested range would run into another
mapping or past the user address space, or memory to back it could not be
committed.
.SH NOTES
The C library's
.BR malloc (3)
allocates with
.BR mmap (2)
and does not use the program break, so a program that mixes the two only
has to keep its own
.BR sbrk ()
calls consistent.
.P
Shrinking the break releases only pages the process maps alone: after
.BR fork (2),
a heap page the other process still maps stays allocated until both have
given it up.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fork (2),
.BR mmap (2),
.BR malloc (3)
